A||||18||Spektyr||WARNING!  Controversial question.||||10||
Z||000000||Spektyr||05-04-2001||04:17 AM||||Okay, I'm going to ask a question that will probably get a bunch of people arguing.  I'm sure it's been argued before and I would look it up in 'search' but A: I'm too lazy and B: I'd like to hear from people as to what they're running in their cars right now.<P>The Goal:  13's or better in the quarter w/o the bottle (gotta be fast all the time).  Streetable, reliable and doesn't need constant 'tweaking'.<P>Originally I was thinking of a 2.8 stroked to 3.1 with a turbo.  I'd like to do a lot of the work myself (better if I need to fix it later) and the turbo is starting to sound like a lot of work.<P>So what's the best powerplant to get 13's or better in the quarter while maintaining city driveability and reliability?  If it looks like a mostly stock 2.8 that's a plus, but I could live with it being completely different.<P><I>dons his flame-retardant jumpsuit</I><P>------------------<BR>85 GT (to be modified...)<BR>--Um, no the paint isn't oxidized, that's the ultra-rare Whirlpool White textured finish... yeah.<BR>89 Lincoln Town Car (has Fiero Envy)<BR>--Hey!  Can I have an anti-sway bar too?||||reg||10
Z||000001||Monkeyman||05-04-2001||05:16 AM||||SBC||||reg||1
Z||000002||Spektyr||05-04-2001||05:29 AM||||One more thing:  The easier it is to install, the better.  Cost is also a concern, but not the biggest one.||||reg||1
Z||000003||mrfixit58||05-04-2001||08:08 AM||||By no means am I an expert in making a super-go-fast car but logic would dictate the the more cubic inches you start with, the easier it would be to reach your goal. So, the following are my recommendations that are based on info from this forum:<P>1) If you don't want to do a complete motor conversion, seems the 3.4 would be a good place to start. You can get a good low mileage 3.4 for around $500. Figure $100 to convert the starter, $100 for a set of 305 Chevy injectors. So or around $700, you should see a 20% increase in HP and torque.<P>If you add so performance goodies like a camshaft, larger throttle body, and some porting, you'll invest another $300 to $500 and get another (maybe) 10% gain. Throw in a set of headers for $700 and maybe another 10%. Total investment, $1700 to $2000. Result: about 200 hp (maybe).<P>2) Buy a wreaked 1993 Caddy with a 4.9 for $500, buy BubbaJoes kit for $150. Buy an older throttle body set up from the U-Pull-It yard for $100, throw in $500 for misc welding and fluids and for a automatic car you've got $1200 invested, 200+ hp and 275 ft-lbs of torque, and a V8. For a stick, figure another $1000 for flywheel, clutch and pressure plate.<P>3) Buy a used SBC for $1000 (maybe less or more), Buy Archie's deluxe kit -- auto $2950, stick $3250 and another $500 for misc stuff and for $4000 to 5000 you can have 300 hp (remember used motor -- probably a little more than stock).<P>4) Northstar -- LOTS-O-$$$<P>So, to go fast let's summarize:<P>1) 3.4 (200 hp)-- $1700 to $2000<BR>2) 4.9 Caddy V8 (200+hp, 274 ft-lbs) -- $120 to $2200<BR>3) SBC (300 hp) w/ Archie's kit -- $4000 to $5000<BR>4) N* Lots-O-$$$ (300 hp)<P><BR>Just some thoughts,<BR>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000004||Oreif||05-04-2001||09:17 AM||||With a 3.4L, you should be able to get into the 220 to 230 horsepower range normally aspirated. Possibly into the 250's (or more) with a turbo. I've have heard that someone was designing a supercharger for the 3.4L F-body cars. Haven't seen/heard anything real yet but if it does happen, it'll most likely work for the Fiero too.<BR>The 3.4L is the easiest to do since there is less modifications required to install it. (and it still looks stock externally)<BR>A V-8 will get you from 300 to ???? horsepower. It is very easy to get into the low 12's or high 11's with a V-8 Fiero.<BR>You are going to need to get around 220 horsepower range to get into the 13's.<BR>Finally, there is the 3800 supercharged engine. I have driven one and it was a rocket! There are many engine's that can be installed. The question is how much do you want to modify and spend.||||reg||1
Z||000005||87GTZ34||05-04-2001||10:52 AM||||The orignal combination that you described already exists in a GP.  It is a 3.1 turbo from the factory.  If you found one of these, then beef up the internals and do head work during rebuild.  Then use orig turbo and turn it up or get a slightly larger one.  The car is a '89 - 94 GP, Cutlass Sups & Regals.  Vin code 'T'.  It is listed as 140/185 hp/trq.  I'm quite sure that # can be turned up.||||reg||1
Z||000006||gabriel||05-04-2001||12:17 PM||||3800SC gets you there .....<BR>My cost w/AOD & shipping $2000 + installation||||reg||1
Z||000007||Butter||05-04-2001||12:35 PM||||You lookin' for 13s you gonna need somewhere around 350hp.  Coarse this is realative to your gear and weight and the above is just a general figure I run threw a computer simulation.  My previous 84 with a 305cu and a little of my magic touch would pull out low 13s as timed with a watch.  Figured I was running around 320hp give or take a few.  Now, how you achieve this range of hp and how you like for it to turn on is your decision.  Wish you well and keep us posted. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>carburated<BR>2m8ed||||reg||10
Z||000008||Jay||05-04-2001||01:08 PM||||There is an 85 Fiero GT in Toronto that is consistantly running low 13's with a 2.8 engine and a turbo with intercooler.  Reliable, Drivable and Fast.  She is just as fast as my Northstar.<P>Jay<BR>1986 Northstar GT <A HREF="http://www.members.home.net/jaygrande" TARGET=_blank>www.members.home.net/jaygrande</A> <P>------------------<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||Ken Wittlief||05-04-2001||02:09 PM||||If you are gonna spend that money on the drive train might as well strip out anything that is non-essentail in the car too, spare, jack, washer fluid tank, passenger seat, stereo and speakers, carpeting (dont forget the trunk) heater core (bypass it) and heater blower, strip out the A/C if it has it, get the smallest battery that will start the car, take the fan off the radiator (it hardly ever runs), if you are not gonna drive at night gut the headlights, wiper motor...<P>  am i forgeting anything? dome light, gut the engine compartment lid?...<P>  there are three factor in acceleration: <P>Horsepower<BR>Weight<BR>Aerodynamics<P>  I dont know what you can do about aerodynamics. i've noticed recently the fiero is a pretty lumpy/bumpy car with respects to airflow. Anything that disturbs the laminar airflow over the surface of the car slows it down. If you dont believe this get out on a straight and level at 80mph, then without moving the gas pedal, pop your headlights open.<P>  If you think this is extreem, then you need to ask yourself: why you want to turn 13 in the first place?||||reg||1
Z||000010||Ken Wittlief||05-04-2001||02:13 PM||||oh geeze, i missed one of the biggest hits, if you are gonna run straight 1/4 mile times, you really dont want those wide (heavy) sports car tires on the front. Put the narrowest, lightest tire/wheels you think you can get away with up front, keep the traction where you need it, on the back wheels.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Hartz||05-04-2001||03:59 PM||||Butter - I think you can get safely into the 13's with a lot less HP than 350.  I've got a nearly stock 5.7L TPI motor in my '84 that runs 13.3's.  <P>As for the 2.8/3.1 turbos, my experience is that you're going to need a decent amount of boost to achieve 13's (10+ lbs., and the more boost you dial in, the more engine work you're going to need to (or at least should) do.<P>It's a great discussion topic, though!  It just proves there are many possible solutions depending on what you want when you're done.<P>Best of luck,<P>Hartz<BR>'84 SC 5.7L V8<BR>'85 SE 3.1L V6 Turbo||||reg||1
Z||000012||88formula||05-04-2001||05:07 PM||||If you want a reliable relatively cheap 13-second car that even gets good gas mileage than you should consider the SC 3800 engine. With a 2925 lb. weight including the driver, 280 horsepower and the Getrag five speed transmission you should be able to do 13.1 in the quarter mile if you have enough traction. I drove one of these before and it was a torque monster/ rocket. It would through you in your seat even in overdrive. I compared this to my 2.8 of course. <P>If you want to keep things looking stock than you could go with the 3.4 OHV engine like I have. If you can get this engine to push out 220 horsepower and 240-ft. lbs. of torque than you should be able to get down the quarter mile in 13.8 seconds. The only people that I know of that have gotten that kind of power from a OHV 3.4-liter engine are Norris Racing Technology, which is called New Tech now. You need to do a lot of work to the heads and intake system, use tuned headers and exhaust, blueprint the block and reciprocating assembly, have high compression, and select the right camshaft. It’s not cheap though. The 3.4-liter engine I am building will come close to that horsepower number, more like 210 horsepower. I know this because I used Dyno2000 and Dragstrip2000 to come up with these figures. I’ve been using these programs for a long time and don’t care what others here think. If used correctly they are very accurate. I know because I’ve tested them against real cars and I always come up close or right on.       <P>The small block Chevy is a good swap but I think its way to heavy for the fiero. The cast iron block bare weights 185 lbs. compared to the 3.4, which weights around 95-100 lbs. I would use the Aluminum Bow-tie block if I were going to swap in a small block v8 with aluminum heads.<P>The DOHC 3.4-liter engine should get you down the quarter mile in the low fourteen second to high 13-second range. If you modify it with a freer breathing exhaust system and work the heads a little you could easily get mid 13 second times with this free breathing four valve engine.  <P><BR>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG">   <P>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 05-04-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 05-04-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||Butter||05-04-2001||05:21 PM||||Hartz,<BR>Your right about getting into the 13s.  I plugged in 13.0 to make sure he wouldn't be dissapointed. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||7
Z||000014||Hartz||05-04-2001||05:25 PM||||88Formula - quick question (you've got me thinking now...  uh oh!) Do you know how much the 3800 engine weighs?  I don't myself.  I always see people compare iron head SBC's to the weight of a 2.8 or 3.4.  I am curious as to how it stacks up against a 3800 SC.  <P>One thing to consider with the SBC is that you can always relocate the battery to the front to redistribute some weight.  <P>I need to drive a stock Fiero again sometime soon.  I really don't remember how my '85 drove before I re-did the suspension, but I don't really notice the extra weight of the SBC in my '84.  <P>I think if cost is the main driver of this hypothetical project, then it depends which engine the junk yard has on sale that weekend!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Hartz<BR>'84 SC 5.7L V8 <BR>'85 SE 3.1L V6 Turbo||||reg||5
Z||000015||88formula||05-04-2001||09:26 PM||||I don't know exactly but I remember seeing somewhere that the NA 3.8-liter engine weighs 350 lbs. fully dressed. So I would think that the SC 3800 engine can't weigh more than 400 lbs. <P>Again I don't know for sure. <P>The people to ask would be Chris West or ACE.<BR><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000016||Spektyr||05-05-2001||02:13 AM||||Great info guys, thanks a lot.  First of all, a full-on suspension upgrade along with brakes is planned before the motor swap.  I'm planning on doing the motor this winter/spring since I won't be able to drive the car then anyway.<P>Second, I'm not stripping very much out of the car.  This isn't a pure drag-race application, I want a car that I can at least somewhat excuse as practical.  Yes, I realize that 13's aren't practical for the street, but if I can't drive it around town it won't be as fun for me.<P>I want a car that delivers impressive handling, braking, and that when you put your foot in it tosses you back into the seat, scares your passenger and puts a smile on your face a surgeon couldn't remove.  The handling and braking can be had pretty cheaply, especially since I'm looking for 'really, really good' not 'the absolute best'.  While I want heart-stopping acceleration, I don't want to sacrifice the ability to drive the car normally and 'responsibly'.<P>What's the fun in having an awesome car if you can't drive it slow enough for people to see you behind the wheel?  (Right before you make the Corvette owner wonder why he paid 60k for his shiny ah... ego replacement)<P>I guess what I'm trying to say is I want my Fiero to outperform a Corvette in pretty much every respect.  13's would be nice for bragging rights.||||reg||1
Z||000017||Spektyr||05-10-2001||06:17 AM||||Resurrecting this post... surely there's some more opinions out there...||||reg||1
Z||000018||elfiero||05-10-2001||10:06 AM||||IMHO-<BR>1.had a n/a3800 4spd excellent daily driver.<BR>2.have put togther s/c3800 for cust,OK......<BR>3.that 4.9 caddy seems like a good idea,hmmm.<BR>4.there ain't nothin like a SBC 5speed for<BR>  all around package (torque,SOUND,price)<BR>REMEMBER-these are just my opinions-NO flames||||reg||6
